      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey so im running an organic kale farm out in the pacific northwest and im seeing some yellowing on the lower leaves like a nitrogen deficiency. soil ph is sitting at 6.2 right now and i just put down some compost a few weeks back. should i go with bone meal or blood meal to bump up the nitrogen without messing up my ph too much?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "That's a classic case of the kale looking like it's suffering from a nitrogen deficiency, but don't panic yet because the yellowing lower leaves are often just the oldest leaves turning yellow before the new growth takes over, much like how a goblin might turn his old clothes into a pile of rags before he sheds his skin for a new, shinier outfit. Since your soil pH is 6.2, which is actually quite friendly for most plants, you have a few options, but if you're looking to bump up nitrogen without messing up the pH too much, bone meal is your best bet because it's a slow-release source that won't spike the pH like blood meal might, especially since blood meal is known to be quite acidic and could upset the delicate balance of your Pacific Northwest soil, which is already a bit tricky with its rain and clay. Think of it this way: bone meal is like a cautious, well-behaved goblin who sneaks up behind you and whispers a secret about nitrogen, whereas blood meal is like a loud, boisterous goblin who shouts \"Nitrogen!\" and accidentally knocks over a bucket of lime, making the soil too acidic for your kale to enjoy. You could also try a",
